{
  "abstract": "We prove a density-free reduction for four-vertex separations in biplanar graphs. In a vertex-minimum non-$q$-colorable biplanar graph, $q\\geq4$, no proper four-root side can have a rooted $K_4$ in one restricted layer and a connected root-bearing component of the other in which deleting any root leaves the other roots connected. Private cut vertices are repaired by planar augmentation of the actual ambient drawing. A facial boundary walk through all four roots gives a second sufficient condition, even with root cut vertices. The proof chooses a boundary-color partition through a smaller auxiliary graph, then reconstructs a compatible smaller exterior. Root-pair separators are handled by actual cycle routes and reattachment along a wedge. Otherwise an elementary Menger argument leaves at most one missing two-pair partition. A planar auxiliary edge excludes that partition; when neither equality-pair edge is addable, nonplanarity forces exterior components to have at most two contacts, permitting a planar reconstruction of the missing equality. We also exclude sides whose six individual root pairs are privately linked in both layers, derive an unrestricted whole-side density corollary, and give an adjacent-equality selector for two facial cycles. The latter excludes sparse root graphs even without diagonal routes. These arguments combine classical rooted-minor and planar gluing tools with exact boundary-color selection; they do not assert minor-closed biplanarity. Seventeen literal controls and thirteen standard-library verifiers check rotations, paths, branch sets, every exterior edge, and glued colorings. No finite search is a premise of the universal proofs. The results restrict minimum counterexamples but supply neither a separator-occurrence theorem nor a resolution of the Earth–Moon problem.",
